<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<kml xmlns="http://www.opengis.net/kml/2.2">
  <Document>
    <name>🌊 LIVE WAVE HEIGHT</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<p><b>Legend</b><br><img src="https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/wave_height/legend.png" width="600" alt="legend"><br>Wave height (feet, 18 fixed colors): <b>0 ft</b> → <b>0.5 ft</b> → <b>1-2 ft</b> → <b>3-4 ft</b> → <b>4-5 ft</b> → <b>6-7 ft</b> → <b>8-9 ft</b> → <b>10-11 ft</b> → <b>12-13 ft</b> → <b>14-15 ft</b> → <b>16-17 ft</b> → <b>18-19 ft</b> → <b>20-21 ft</b> → <b>22-23 ft</b> → <b>24-25 ft</b> → <b>26-27 ft</b> → <b>28-29 ft</b> → <b>30+ ft</b> (white). Every pixel shows its bin's exact color; the legend is the full table. Unwritten rules: <b>0 and 0.1 ft</b> show as <b>0 ft</b>; <b>0.2 to 0.6 ft</b> show as <b>0.5 ft</b>; <b>0.7 to 1 ft</b> show as <b>1-2 ft</b>. Each 2-ft label covers its full step up to the next row (e.g. the 4-5 ft color covers 4 up to 6 ft), so every value lands in exactly one color. Model maximum this run: <b>7.8 ft</b>. Values above 30 ft stay white. Significant height = average of highest third of waves. For more precise wave height data, turn on the NOAA Great Lakes Observation Network layer: its buoys report observed wave height at their exact locations, while this layer shows the wave model everywhere. How the buoys measure it: significant wave height (WVHT) is the average of the highest one-third of the waves recorded during a 20-minute sampling period each hour. Accelerometers on the hull record its heave motion, an onboard FFT converts that motion into a wave-energy spectrum, and the height is derived from that spectrum; buoy reports cover combined wind waves and swell. Great Lakes buoys are seasonal and are hauled out before ice season, so winter gaps are normal.</p><p>This entry auto-refreshes from the live overlay (source: NOAA/NCEP Great Lakes Wave Unstructured v2.1 (GLWUv2.1, WAVEWATCH III)).</p><p>Turn on/off independently of temperature and ice layers. Add this file once; new source cycles appear automatically.</p><p><i>Not endorsed by NOAA.</i></p>]]></description>
    <NetworkLink>
      <name>🌊 LIVE WAVE HEIGHT — auto-refresh</name>
      <Link>
        <href>https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/kml/live/Great_Lakes_Live_Wave_Height.kml</href>
        <refreshMode>onInterval</refreshMode>
        <refreshInterval>900</refreshInterval>
      </Link>
    </NetworkLink>
  </Document>
</kml>
